### Manual Testing Flow

1. **Register new user:**
```
   POST /auth/register
   Body: { email, password, name }
   Expected: 201 with tokens
```

2. **Login with created user:**
```
   POST /auth/login
   Body: { email, password }
   Expected: 200 with tokens
```

3. **Access protected endpoint:**
```
   GET /api/user/profile
   Header: Authorization: Bearer {accessToken}
   Expected: 200 with user data
```

4. **Wait for token expiration (or manipulate expiry time):**
```
   GET /api/user/profile
   Header: Authorization: Bearer {expired accessToken}
   Expected: 401 Unauthorized
```

5. **Refresh token:**
```
   POST /auth/refresh
   Body: { refreshToken }
   Expected: 200 with new accessToken
```

6. **Logout:**
```
   POST /auth/logout
   Header: Authorization: Bearer {accessToken}
   Expected: 200 success message
```

7. **Try to refresh with logged-out token:**
```
   POST /auth/refresh
   Body: { old refreshToken }
   Expected: 403 Token not found

7. Security Considerations
Password Security
Use BCrypt with cost factor 10-12
Enforce minimum password length (8 characters)
Require password complexity: uppercase, lowercase, number, special char
Never log passwords
Never return passwords in API responses
Token Security
Use strong secret key (256-bit minimum)
Store secret in environment variable, never hardcode
Use secure random generators for refresh tokens
Set appropriate expiration times (short for access, longer for refresh)
Implement token rotation on refresh
Consider token blacklisting for logout
API Security
Always use HTTPS in production
Implement rate limiting on auth endpoints
Add account lockout after failed login attempts
Log authentication events for monitoring
Validate all inputs
Sanitize error messages (don't leak information)
Database Security
Never store plaintext passwords
Use prepared statements (JPA does this automatically)
Implement database connection pooling
Restrict database user permissions
Regular security audits

8. Common Pitfalls to Avoid
Weak JWT Secret:
Don't use short secrets (minimum 256 bits)
Don't hardcode secrets in code
Token in URL:
Never pass tokens in URL query parameters
Always use Authorization header
No Token Expiration:
Always set expiration on access tokens
Set reasonable expiration on refresh tokens
Returning Password in Response:
Exclude password field from User JSON serialization
Use @JsonIgnore on password field
Not Validating Input:
Always use @Valid annotation
Implement custom validators for complex rules
Insufficient Error Handling:
Catch specific exceptions
Return appropriate HTTP status codes
Don't expose stack traces in production
CORS Misconfiguration:
Don't allow all origins in production
Specify exact allowed origins
Configure allowed methods and headers properly
Session Management:
Ensure STATELESS session management
Don't mix session and JWT authentication

